# Fault-library guest kernel: retain the pvclock but let ring-3 read the
# timestamp counter.
#
# The historical database specimens cannot run under CR4.TSD. Go's runtime
# reads the counter directly (etcd and etcdctl fault on their first
# `cputicks`), and so does glibc's dynamic loader before `main`, which takes
# down every PostgreSQL binary. CR4.TSD faults inside the guest before any VM
# exit can happen, so the host's RDTSC exiting is never reached while it is
# set.
#
# This variant is therefore only deterministic on a host with the patched KVM
# loaded, where RDTSC exits and the host answers from virtual time. On stock
# KVM it reads the raw host counter and is fit for smoke tests only.
#
# Distinct from x86-n6-traps-off-config-fragment, which carries the same symbol
# for an unrelated purpose: that kernel is the planted negative that proves a
# hostile JIT probe can detect a live counter, and its identity stays
# unambiguous.
# CONFIG_HARMONY_USER_COUNTER_TRAPS is not set
#
# Single processor. The guest has one vCPU, and the host delivers its timer
# interrupt only at VM exits it services. On an SMP kernel a writer that finds
# an rwsem reserved for a queued waiter spins until the scheduler tick, and a
# spinning guest never exits, so the tick never comes and the guest hangs;
# three processes writing one tmpfs file reach that state. Without SMP there
# is no optimistic spinning: the writer queues, the guest idles, and the
# host jumps to the timer.
# CONFIG_SMP is not set
#
# The task park (patches/x86/0006): a workload thread held at a user
# instruction by a hardware execution breakpoint the kernel owns, for the
# races no signal-delivered pause can reach. Needs the perf breakpoint
# framework the Kata base provides.
CONFIG_HARMONY_PARK=y
